{
  "_comment": "TEMPLATE for tests/manifest.local.json — copy this file to that name (it is gitignored) and replace every <placeholder>. It carries the values that describe YOUR server, YOUR TV and YOUR library; tests/manifest.json carries only the case definitions, which are the same everywhere. run.py merges this over the manifest at load and refuses to run without it. It does NOT refuse over an `items` key it cannot resolve: leave a shape you do not own bracketed and the cases needing it are SKIPPED, with the reason printed in the summary and in --list. Everything else here is still fatal when left as a placeholder, because no run of any size can proceed without it. No secret belongs here either - the access token is still read from the gitignored src/config.local.h at runtime. NB none of this is needed for the DEFAULT tier: a bare `./tests/run.py` plays generated clips served off your own machine, with no server, no token and no item ids - it needs a TV address and nothing else, and takes it from the repo's gitignored .tv-host if this file does not exist at all. This file is what `./tests/run.py --server` needs. See tests/README.md, 'Two tiers'.",
  "server": {
    "host": "<server-host>",
    "port": 8096
  },
  "tv": "<tv-host>",
  "_flavour_comment": "WHICH INSTALL the harness drives: `stable` (com.butaca, the app users install) or `debug` (com.butaca.debug, the developer build beside it, with its own launcher tile, its own sign-in and its own runtime files). OPTIONAL -- delete the key and run.py falls back to the Makefile's own default, which is `debug`; --flavor overrides it for one invocation. It decides everything downstream: which app id is closed and launched, which runtime root the triggers and the injected token are written into (/tmp for stable, /tmp/<app id> otherwise), and which event log is graded -- run.py asks the Makefile for each of those (`make -s print-appid/print-rundir/print-eventlog FLAVOR=<f>`) instead of restating them. In practice this stays `debug`: the harness needs a build with the dev triggers compiled in, and `make deploy` refuses to put one on the stable id without ALLOW_DEV_ON_STABLE=1. A run against a release build reads none of its triggers, so it plays nothing and every case fails as if the player were broken -- run.py aborts on the app's `install:` boot line rather than let that happen. Not validated here: the Makefile's FLAVORS whitelist is the one list, and a wrong value fails with its parse-time error naming the allowed set.",
  "flavour": "debug",
  "_test_user_comment": "By default the harness signs in as this user, so test playback and timeline updates land on THIS user's history, never the owner's real account. run.py signs in with these credentials (or uses `token` when you give it one) and stores no secret. Pass --owner to run as the config.local.h owner token instead. The user must already have the libraries shared with it. Delete this block entirely to always run as the owner (run.py warns that watch history WILL be affected).",
  "test_user": {
    "user": "<username>",
    "password": "<password, or omit this and set `token` instead>"
  },
  "_items_comment": "Maps each symbolic `item` key used by tests/manifest.json to the Id of an item on YOUR server with that shape. The key names the shape the case needs, and each case's `title` in manifest.json spells it out in full, so a missing or wrong mapping is a test-setup error rather than a mystery failure - run.py names the key it could not resolve. Several cases deliberately SHARE one item, which is why run.py resets the server-side resume point before every case. Three keys are for the FPS scenes rather than a playback case and carry an extra requirement: `movie_in_home_catalog` must be reachable from the home catalog (recently-added / on-deck) or detail-transition never enters route=detail, `movie_cast0_in_both_libraries` must have a first-billed cast member with titles in BOTH libraries so the person page draws two poster shelves, and `show_detail` must be a SHOW (not an episode or season) with at least one season of episodes, so show-detail draws the season strip and the episode shelf - the more episodes in its first season, the more the scene stresses. `collection` is the one key that names a COLLECTION rather than a playable item: it must be a movie collection with at least two members that have posters, and it must live in the Movies library the library scenes enter (your first/preferred one), because library-collections sweeps that library's collections and collection-page draws its members. `collection_member_movie` (detail-collection-shelf) must be a movie with extras and cast that belongs to a collection with at least two members, so its detail page draws the collection shelf where detailsec=3 lands. An item whose shape you have nothing for should be LEFT BRACKETED (or deleted): the cases and fps scenes that name it are then skipped by name, with the reason, and everything else runs. Do not map a near-miss to fill the slot -- a case that passes on the wrong shape hides the bug it exists to catch.",
  "items": {
    "movie_h264_ac3_1080p": "<item id>",
    "episode_hevc_4k_hdr10_eac3": "<item id>",
    "episode_hevc_4k_hdr10_eac3_next": "<item id: the episode that follows the one above>",
    "movie_hevc_4k_hdr10_truehd": "<item id>",
    "movie_hevc_4k_dovi_p8": "<item id>",
    "movie_hevc_aac_mp4": "<item id>",
    "episode_h264_aac": "<item id>",
    "movie_h264_ac3_many_audio": "<item id>",
    "movie_av1_no_dp_audio": "<item id>",
    "movie_hevc_4k_pgs_subs": "<item id>",
    "movie_in_home_catalog": "<item id>",
    "show_detail": "<item id: a SHOW, not an episode — ideally one season with many episodes>",
    "movie_cast0_in_both_libraries": "<item id>",
    "collection": "<item id: a movie COLLECTION, not a movie>",
    "collection_member_movie": "<item id>"
  }
}
